{# A computed Main Line Items fee — a BC Environmental Fee, a BC Recycling Fee — rendered as a real
   row in a sell-side document's line table, dash-filled in every column that does not apply to it,
   so the fee shows up next to the products it came from and not only in the totals box below.

   NOT an editable or removable line: it is recomputed from scratch on every save (the order form's
   recalc, EstimateController::recomputeFeesAndTax()), so it carries no line_* inputs and nothing
   here posts.

   Shared by templates/admin/order/form.html.twig and templates/admin/estimate/form.html.twig,
   which had the same row twice under two names (`fee_line_row`, `estimate_fee_line_row`) — same
   signature, same shape, same fee-line-row class, same number_format(2), differing only in how
   many columns their line tables have and whether the cells carry a data-label.

   Parameters
   ----------
   rowClass        required  the document's own line-row class — 'order-line-row' or
                             'estimate-line-row'. It pairs with `fee-line-row`, which is added
                             here and is what both forms' CSS and both suites' assertions key on.
   label           required  the fee's name, in the first cell
   amount          required  the money, in the `subtotalColumn` cell, always number_format(2)
   columns         required  the line table's columns in order. Its LENGTH is how many cells the
                             row spans — an order's line table has 14, a quote's 13, because an
                             order carries a Batch column a quote has no use for — and its entries
                             are the data-label each cell gets when `labelled`.
   subtotalColumn  required  which entry of `columns` carries the amount; every other cell that is
                             not the first is a dash. A name and not an index, so the caller states
                             the column rather than counting to it.
   labelled        required  true to put `data-label` on every cell, for the responsive
                             stacked-table CSS that reads it. The quote does; the order does not,
                             anywhere in its line table, which is a drift worth closing on its own
                             and not from inside this row.
   taxCodeColumn   optional  which entry of `columns` carries `taxCode` — a fee has one (its
                             FeeLine::$taxClass) same as a product line does, so it is stated
                             rather than dashed (#667). Omitted columns keep dashing, same as
                             before this parameter existed.
   taxCode         optional  the fee's own tax class ('E'/'G'/'S'), plain text — never an editable
                             select, unlike a product line's: a fee is recomputed from scratch on
                             every save (see the note above) and has nothing to post one into.
   taxAmountColumn optional  which entry of `columns` carries `taxAmount`.
   taxAmount       optional  this fee's own tax, computed in isolation from the rest of the
                             document (OrderTaxBreakdownService's perFeeLineTax, #667) — not a
                             share of the merged GST/PST row the totals box shows, which is a sum
                             across every taxable line and fee and would overstate what THIS row
                             alone charged.

   The dash is core's literal em dash rather than `&mdash;`: the quote's copy spelled it as the
   entity and the order's as the character, the same character either way, and the literal is what
   the rest of the templates use. #}
{% macro fee_line_row(rowClass, label, amount, columns, subtotalColumn, labelled, taxCodeColumn, taxCode, taxAmountColumn, taxAmount) %}
    <tr class="{{ rowClass }} fee-line-row">
{% for column in columns %}
        <td{% if labelled %} data-label="{{ column }}"{% endif %}>{% if loop.first %}{{ label }}{% elseif column == subtotalColumn %}${{ amount|amount }}{% elseif taxCodeColumn is defined and column == taxCodeColumn %}{{ taxCode|default('') ?: '—' }}{% elseif taxAmountColumn is defined and column == taxAmountColumn %}${{ taxAmount|default(0)|amount }}{% else %}—{% endif %}</td>
{% endfor %}
    </tr>
{% endmacro %}
